28. Tool execution architecture
The UI should be decoupled from individual tools.

Instead of:

self.buttons["sast"] = ...
self.buttons["secrets"] = ...
self.buttons["entropy"] = ...
self.buttons["typosquat"] = ...

the UI should listen to agent events.

Conceptually:

agent.on("thinking", ...)
agent.on("tool_started", ...)
agent.on("tool_progress", ...)
agent.on("tool_completed", ...)
agent.on("finding", ...)
agent.on("agent_delegated", ...)
agent.on("workflow_started", ...)
agent.on("workflow_completed", ...)
agent.on("error", ...)

Then the UI automatically renders those events.

This is critical.

29. Introduce an Agent Event Bus
Create something like:

class AgentEvent:
    type: str
    timestamp: datetime
    session_id: str
    tool: str | None
    agent: str | None
    status: str
    message: str
    data: dict

Examples:

AgentEvent(
    type="tool_started",
    tool="static_analysis",
    status="running"
)

Then:

AgentEvent(
    type="tool_completed",
    tool="static_analysis",
    status="success",
    data={
        "findings": 3,
        "duration": 1.24
    }
)

The UI consumes these events.

This will make the interface dramatically easier to evolve.

30. Threading/UI safety
The current application has another architectural problem:

many worker threads directly manipulate Tkinter widgets.

For example:

threading.Thread(target=task).start()

and then inside the thread:

self.log(...)
self._set_ui_state(...)

Tkinter is not thread-safe.

Move all UI updates back onto the main thread using:

root.after(...)

or introduce an event queue:

queue.Queue()

Worker:

event_queue.put(event)

UI:

root.after(50, process_events)

This should be part of the redesign.

